This section's raw extraction has several defects, all upstream OpenStax
content quirks reproduced faithfully by the extractor rather than
converter bugs:

- The converter's raw output writes every x-/y-axis mention as
  `$x\text{-axis}$`/`$y\text{-axis}$`, and one instance (the
  integrate-over-x-or-y-axis instruction) as plain unmathed prose
  "x-axis or y-axis" with no math at all. Normalized every occurrence to
  `$x$-axis`/`$y$-axis`, per this skill's own convention.
- Nearly every exercise's second and later curve/condition is joined with
  `\,\text{and}\,` inside a single $...$ span, trapping the English word
  "and" in math mode (and, in three items, comma-separated equation lists
  fused the same way). This file's own exercise 3 ("$y=x^{3}$ and
  $y=x^{2}+x$") already uses the correct split form, so the fused form is
  an internal inconsistency, not just a stylistic nit. Rewrote every such
  item to close the math span before "and" and reopen it after, following
  that sibling exercise's own convention.
- Exercise 1's figure alt text is missing a space: "x^2-3and g(x)" ->
  "x^2-3 and g(x)".
- Exercise 2's figure alt text says the region is "bounded above by g(x)
  and below by g(x)" -- both bounds name the same curve. For f(x)=x^2 and
  g(x)=3x+4 (their point of comparison here), g(x) is the upper curve
  between the intersection points, matching every sibling figure
  description in this file ("bounded above by g(x) and below by f(x)").
  Corrected the lower bound to f(x).
- Seven figure alt texts read "This figure is has two/the graph...",
  a grammatical glitch in the source's own boilerplate template.
  Corrected to "This figure has...".
- Exercise 61 (the cell-phone factory problem)'s prose runs "selling x
  cell phonesFind the area" with no space or sentence break. Corrected to
  "cell phones. Find the area".

